      "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nTate's continuous cohomology (<REF_0001>) is defined by the complex of\ncontinuous inhomogeneous cochains. We can define this when <MATH_0001> is an\narbitrary topological abelian group endowed with a continuous <MATH_0002>-action.\nNamely, we consider the complex\n<MATH_0003>\nwhere the boundary map is defined for <MATH_0004> by the rule\n<MATH_0005>\nand for <MATH_0006> sends <MATH_0007> to the map <MATH_0008>. We define\n<MATH_0009>\nSince the terms of the complex involve continuous maps from <MATH_0010> and\nself products of <MATH_0011> into the topological module <MATH_0012>, it is not clear\nthat this turns a short exact sequence of topological modules into\na long exact cohomology sequence. Another difficulty is that the category\nof topological abelian groups isn't an abelian category!\n\n<STRUCT_0002><STRUCT_0003>\nHowever, a short exact sequence of discrete <MATH_0013>-modules does give\nrise to a short exact sequence of complexes of continuous cochains\nand hence a long exact cohomology sequence of continuous\ncohomology groups <MATH_0014>.\nTherefore, on the category <MATH_0015> of\nDefinition <REF_0002> the functors\n<MATH_0016> form a cohomological <MATH_0017>-functor as defined\nin Homology, Section <REF_0003>.\nSince the cohomology <MATH_0018>\nof Definition <REF_0004>\nis a universal <MATH_0019>-functor\n(Derived Categories, Lemma <REF_0005>)\nwe obtain canonical maps\n<MATH_0020>\nfor <MATH_0021>. It is known that these maps are\nisomorphisms when <MATH_0022> is an abstract group (i.e., <MATH_0023> has\nthe discrete topology) or when <MATH_0024> is a profinite group\n(insert future reference here).\nIf you know an example showing this map is not an isomorphism\nfor a topological group <MATH_0025> and <MATH_0026>\nplease email\n<TEXTOPEN_0001>stacks.project@gmail.com<TEXTCLOSE_0001>.\n\n\n\n\n",
      "source_text_hash": "sha256:d3eb7ae78c4b00118563604b483a3c736296fe566b25cf41f10041d26e81001e",
      "unit_id": "tag:0DVG:prose-0001"
    }
  ],
